# Issue: specify and design `cador`, the headless multidocument CAD host

- **Status:** open — specification and design work, no production code beyond the mechanical migration in §7
- **Depends on:** `clautolisp-multidocument-analysis.org` (requirements R1–R21, experiments E1–E10)
- **Related:** `clautolisp-debugger-remote-cad-spec.org` (Revision 2), `issue-encoding-dispatch.md`
- **Blocks:** any further entity-level work, currently mislocated in the null host

## 0. Sub-tasks (decomposition, 2026-08-06)

This epic is decomposed into ordered sub-issues (owner's breakdown):

- **cador-1-split-nihil-cador** (P1, NEXT, branch `cador`) — name the two
  headless hosts: `nihil` = the stub host (today `null-host`, already
  stubs; make its stubs warn "not implemented in this host"); `cador` =
  the real headless CAD core (today `mock-host` — that is where the
  accumulated CAD functions actually live, not the null host; §7's
  "null host accumulated" framing pre-dates the mock-host extraction).
  Implement immediately.
- **cador-2-multidocument-runtime** (P1, branch `cador-multidocument`, in
  PARALLEL) — N isolated document namespaces/engines/programs, one shared
  blackboard on one shared heap (no copy), per-document drawing;
  cooperative single-runner coroutine scheduler (one thread/document used
  as a coroutine, aldo-style; one aldo per document ⇒ ~2 threads/document).
- **cador-3-host-interface-protocol-spec** (P2) — D1 host-interface spec +
  the formal AutoLISP↔host protocol + D2 cador spec + D5 naming note.
- **cador-4-epic-remainder** (P3) — DB backend, command layer, §7.4
  interface adaptation, locking, conformance E1–E10, wire host.

## 1. Decision record

The following is settled. Do not relitigate it in the deliverables; record it as
given and move on.

**The host interface is clautolisp's single way out to CAD and GUI features.**
Everything that is not pure AutoLISP evaluation — documents, databases,
entities, system variables, user interaction, drawing output — crosses this
interface. Nothing else in the interpreter may reach a CAD backend directly.

Three hosts are planned:

| Host | Nature | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `nihil` | No-op stubs | Keeps the interpreter running with no CAD backend at all. Explicitly implements nothing CAD-like. |
| `cador` | Headless, in-process CAD core | Real documents, databases and entities, no GUI. Semantically comparable to AutoCAD and BricsCAD. The analogue of AutoCAD Core Console (`accoreconsole`). |
| *(name TBD)* | Wire protocol to a remote display/editor server | Forwards host operations to a separate CAD/GUI application process that owns the real editor and display. Closer in spirit to Display PostScript or X11 than to a Lisp REPL: clautolisp evaluates, the remote process displays and gathers input. **Not** the `alfe` protocol — see §1.1. |

Consequences:

1. `cador` is a **peer** of the wire host, not a family name covering both. My
   earlier suggestion to make `cador` the umbrella for a host family is
   superseded and should not appear in the deliverables.
2. Entity handling was begun in the null host. It **belongs in `cador`** and
   must be moved there (§7).
3. The null host is renamed `nihil` and reduced to no-op stubs whose only
   purpose is to prevent crashes. Its contract is *negative*: it must never
   grow a CAD-like implementation. Any pressure to do so is a signal that the
   feature belongs in `cador`.
4. Naming the wire host is a separate decision. Use the placeholder
   `<wire-host>` in the deliverables; do not block on it.

### 1.1 The two wire protocols are distinct

The system contains two unrelated wires. Conflating them, as an earlier draft of
this issue did, corrupts both designs.

**W1 — the `alfe` protocol.** `alfe` exists because AutoLISP inside AutoCAD and
BricsCAD has no terminal. `princ`, `prin1`, `print` and a bare `read-line`
address the CAD's own console — the F2 text window — or files; they never touch
the process's stdin and stdout. `read` is not a stream reader at all: it takes a
string, and is therefore `cl:read-from-string` under another name. There is
consequently no way, from AutoLISP running inside a real product, to obtain a
terminal CLI or REPL. `alfe` supplies one: a terminal front-end that ships
AutoLISP forms to a real AutoCAD or BricsCAD for evaluation by the CAD's own
engine, mediating I/O bidirectionally in mid-evaluation. It may later also carry
a debugger session over the terminal, and forward a channel to Emacs. On W1,
**the CAD evaluates**.

**W2 — the host wire protocol.** The wire host is the mirror image.
**clautolisp evaluates**, and the remote process is a display and editor server
executing host operations on request: make this entity, prompt for that point,
show this. The references are Display PostScript and X11, not SLIME.

`alfe` may also use clautolisp as its backend, which gives a terminal user
access to `nihil`, `cador` and any future host. Those host operations reach the
host **through clautolisp's host interface**, not over W1. The alfe protocol is
not involved, and must not be assumed available, when a clautolisp host is in
play.

### 1.2 Three debugging configurations

The deliverables must keep these apart:

- **(a) Hosted.** Debuggee: clautolisp, in-image. Host: `nihil` or `cador`.
  This is the same-image two-thread design with signal/park handoff.
- **(b) Wire-hosted.** Debuggee: still clautolisp, in-image; the host is a
  peripheral that may block on W2. Poll points remain in clautolisp. New
  hazard to specify: a host call may be outstanding when a break is requested.
- **(c) Remote CAD.** Debuggee: the CAD's *own* AutoLISP engine, reached over
  W1. The instrumentation transform runs in `alfe` using clautolisp's reader,
  and two bodies per function are emitted and shipped. No clautolisp host is
  involved anywhere in this configuration.

E1–E10 establish reference behaviour in the environment of configuration (c) —
a real product — and conformance in configuration (a).

## 2. Required reading before writing anything

- `clautolisp-multidocument-analysis.org` in full. The R-numbers and E-numbers
  below refer to it and must be cited by number in the deliverables.
- The `§4.2` invariant of that document — *one runner, N parked contexts* — is
  the load-bearing constraint of this whole issue.
- The parent debugger spec and its remote-CAD addendum, read with §1.2 in hand.
  The addendum's Part III replacement targets configuration (c) — a real CAD
  over W1 — and is *not* a specification of any clautolisp host. Do not import
  its wire assumptions into D1.

## 3. Deliverables

Write these as separate documents. Do not merge D1 into D2: the interface must
be specifiable without reference to any particular host, or it is not an
interface.

- **D1 — `clautolisp-host-interface-spec.org`**
  The generic host interface every host implements. Backend-agnostic.
- **D2 — `clautolisp-cador-spec.org`**
  Part I: the semantics `cador` presents to AutoLISP code.
  Part II: the internal design realising those semantics.
- **D3 — Migration commits** per §7. Mechanical, reviewable, no behaviour
  change smuggled in.
- **D4 — Conformance test suite skeleton** per §8, with E1–E10 as its first
  cases.
- **D5 — A short naming and architecture note** (may be a section of D1)
  recording §1 of this issue, so the decision survives without this issue.

Conventions: org-mode, `~verbatim~` for Lisp symbols containing asterisks, org
tables, `[[link]]` for URLs. Carry the analysis document's provenance grades
(A/B/C/V) on every factual claim about AutoCAD or BricsCAD behaviour. Flag every
assumption explicitly as an assumption, in a dedicated subsection, until it is
resolved — do not let assumed behaviour read as established behaviour.

## 4. Required structure for D1, the host interface

Specify operations by group. For each operation give: name, arguments, return
value, error conditions, and the expected behaviour of *each* host.

1. **Session and document management** — enumerate documents, create, open,
   close, activate; current versus active document; document identity as a
   first-class value.
2. **Database and entity access** — entity creation, retrieval, modification,
   deletion; entity data lists; symbol tables; dictionaries; extended data.
3. **System variables** — get, set, and the scope tier of each (R4).
4. **Interaction and the console device** — string, integer, real, point, angle
   and keyword input; entity selection; message and prompt output. Specify the
   *console* as an explicit host-provided device, distinct from file streams,
   because AutoLISP's I/O model requires it: `princ`, `prin1`, `print` and a
   bare `read-line` address the console rather than process stdin and stdout,
   and `read` parses a string rather than reading a stream. Do not model the
   console as a `cl:stream` handed to the interpreter. Each host may map the device
   differently: `nihil` and `cador` bind it to its own standard streams
   (~*standard-output*~); a GUI host would display the output in a GUI windows, 
   a wire host would forwards it to the remote editor. This is the
   group that distinguishes the hosts most sharply.
5. **Command layer** — invoking native CAD commands, if at all (§9.4).
6. **Filesystem and encoding** — file streams, code page resolution; must
   interoperate with `issue-encoding-dispatch.md` rather than duplicate it.
7. **Notification** — reactors and events.
8. **Concurrency and locking** — document locking, park/resume, and the
   capability of a host to suspend an evaluation.

Then specify two cross-cutting mechanisms:

- **Capability query.** A host must declare which operations it supports, so
  that library code, `aldo` and the test suite can adapt rather than crash.
  Define the query, its granularity, and whether it is consulted at load time
  or at call time.
- **Condition taxonomy.** Distinguish, as distinct condition classes:
  *operation not supported by this host* (`nihil` asked for an entity),
  *operation unavailable in a headless context* (`cador` asked for a rubber-band
  drag), *operation not yet implemented* (a real gap), and *backend error*
  (the remote CAD refused). Conflating these makes the test suite unable to tell
  a gap from a design boundary.

Close D1 with a **fidelity matrix**: rows are operations, columns are `nihil`,
`cador`, `<wire-host>`, AutoCAD, BricsCAD. This table is the contract, and it is
also the checklist the conformance suite walks.

## 5. Required structure for D2, `cador`

Part I, semantics. Each of these must reach a stated decision, not a survey:

1. **Session model.** N document images, one blackboard environment, one
   application-context image, one session-level subr table (R1).
2. **Scheduler.** The single-runner cooperative model. Specify what parking is,
   where the park points are, what resuming restores, and what happens when a
   host operation is invoked from a parked context (R11, R13).
3. **Current versus active document.** In a headless core "active" has no window
   focus to derive from. Define what it means, or state that the two collapse
   into one notion and why that is safe.
4. **Namespace and symbol model.** Global interning with per-document value and
   function cells, versus per-document obarray (R2). Depends on E1; state the
   choice, state the fallback if E1 contradicts it.
5. **Blackboard.** Value-only, function-rejecting; copy versus share semantics
   (R3, E2).
6. **System variable scope tiers.** Drawing / Registry-equivalent / Not-saved.
   In a headless core the persistent tier needs a concrete home — a config file,
   an environment variable, or explicit non-persistence. Decide (R4, E7).
7. **Handles.** Entity names, selection sets, ldata; document tagging;
   cross-document dereference is an error (R5).
8. **Streams and encoding.** Ownership by document namespace, close-on-close
   behaviour, `DWGCODEPAGE` versus `SYSCODEPAGE` (R6).
9. **Startup hook chain.** The pending-load list that makes `vl-propagate` and
   `vl-load-all` apply to documents opened later (R9), and which of the
   `acad.lsp` / `acaddoc.lsp` / `on_start` / `on_doc_load` chains `cador`
   emulates under each dialect flag.
10. **The console device in a headless core.** Where `cador`'s console reads
    from and writes to; how a script drives it; what happens on a non-tty
    source; whether the device is per-session or per-document. The behaviour of
    `accoreconsole` is the reference point to establish here (§9.1). Specify a
    scripted, non-interactive source as a first-class mode, not a degraded one:
    this is the mode CI depends on.
11. **Deliberate omissions.** An explicit list of what `cador` does not do:
    display, dialogs, real-time cursor tracking, plotting to a device.
    Each omission names the condition class it signals.

Part II, design:

12. **Database backend.** The largest open decision. Present the alternatives —
    a native Common Lisp in-memory database with DXF read/write; a native
    database with no persistence at first; binding an external library for DWG —
    with their consequences for portability, licensing and the fidelity matrix.
    Recommend one, and specify the entity model concretely enough to implement.
13. **Command layer scope.** See §9.4.
14. **Debugger integration.** The concrete surfaces `aldo` attaches to: poll
    points, per-document debugger state, park/signal per context, workspace
    snapshot with scope awareness, breakpoint intent versus materialised filters
    (R16–R21).
15. **Module and package layout.** ASDF systems, package names, the boundary
    that a linter or review can check to prove nothing bypasses the host
    interface.

## 6. Non-negotiable constraints

Any design violating one of these is wrong, not merely different.

- **C1.** No global Lisp environment. N isolated document environments plus the
  blackboard plus the application context (R1, R2).
- **C2.** No concurrent per-document execution. At most one evaluation runs at
  any instant; any number may be parked (§4.2, R13).
- **C3.** The blackboard carries values, never functions (R3).
- **C4.** Every system variable carries its scope tier as data (R4).
- **C5.** Handles are document-tagged; cross-document dereference signals (R5).
- **C6.** No CAD access outside the host interface, from anywhere in the
  interpreter.
- **C7.** `nihil` implements nothing CAD-like. If a test passes against `nihil`
  in a way that implies entity semantics, either the test or `nihil` is wrong.
- **C8.** `cador` needs no worker thread, and must not require one. The
  single-runner model is a simplification to exploit, not an obstacle (R14,
  R21).

## 7. Migration instructions

Ordered. One commit per step, each independently reviewable.

1. **Inventory.** List every entity-related and document-related function
   currently in the null host, with its call sites. Publish the list in the
   issue thread before moving anything.
2. **Create the `cador` system and package**, empty, with its ASDF definition
   and a placeholder host registration.
3. **Move the entity code verbatim.** A pure move: no renaming, no
   reorganisation, no fixes. The diff must be readable as a relocation.
4. **Adapt the moved code** to the D1 interface, in a separate commit, so that
   review can see exactly what changed semantically.
5. **Rename the null host to `nihil`** — package, system, file names, host
   registration, documentation.
6. **Reduce `nihil` to stubs.** Every operation either no-ops or signals the
   *not supported by this host* condition, per the D1 taxonomy. Delete, do not
   comment out, whatever CAD-like remnants survive.
7. **Update the test suite** so that tests requiring entity semantics select
   `cador`, and tests exercising the interpreter core alone select `nihil`. This
   split is itself a useful assertion about the codebase.
8. **Update CI** per §8.

## 8. Conformance tests and CI

- **E1–E10 become conformance probes.** Each experiment in the analysis
  document is a test case runnable against any host. Against AutoCAD and
  BricsCAD they establish reference behaviour; against `cador` they assert
  conformance. Structure the harness so a probe is written once and run
  three ways, with results tabulated per host and version.
- **`cador` is the natural CI host.** Tests needing a CAD backend can select
  clautolisp-with-`cador` instead of a real product, which makes them hermetic.
  The console device (§5.10) is what makes that work: a scripted, non-tty source
  that never touches a terminal. Note the division of responsibility — the
  `isatty` guards, bounded reads, non-interactive `*debugger-hook*` and explicit
  `GITLAB_CI` detection belong to `alfe`'s terminal layer, not to `cador`;
  `cador` contributes a path with no terminal to query in the first place.
  Record in D2 that making the test suite hermetic is part of why the headless
  host exists.
- **The fidelity matrix from D1 is the coverage target.** A cell with no test
  is an untested contract.

## 9. Open questions the specification must resolve explicitly

Do not silently assume any of these. Each gets a subsection, a stated
resolution or a stated assumption, and a provenance grade.

1. **`accoreconsole` fidelity envelope.** What the AutoCAD Core Console
   actually supports — which input primitives function, what happens to
   dialogs, whether plotting works, how it is driven — must be established from
   vendor documentation with citations, not from recollection. `cador` is
   described as its analogue, so the analogy needs a defined boundary.
2. **BricsCAD headless equivalent.** Determine whether Bricsys ships a
   comparable console or headless mode, and under what name. If it does, its
   behaviour is a second reference point; if it does not, say so, because that
   asymmetry affects what "portable" means for `cador`.
3. **Database backend.** §5.12. Decide, with reasons.
4. **Command layer scope.** Does `cador` implement native commands such that
   `(command "LINE" …)` works, or is entity access purely programmatic
   (`entmake`, `entget`, `entmod`)? A minimal command set, none at all, and
   full emulation are three very different projects. State which, and specify
   what `(command …)` and `(command-s …)` do for unimplemented commands. Note
   that a great deal of real AutoLISP code in the wild drives commands rather
   than the entity API, so this decision determines how much existing code
   `cador` can run at all.
5. **Locking semantics in a headless core.** Document locking exists in the
   real products to protect against cross-context modification. With a single
   runner and no user, decide whether `cador` implements locking faithfully,
   implements it as a checked no-op that still detects protocol violations, or
   omits it. The middle option is worth serious consideration: it catches bugs
   that would only otherwise appear against a real CAD backend.
6. **E1 and E2 outcomes** — symbol identity and blackboard sharing. Until
   settled, D2 must state the assumed answer and the cost of being wrong.
7. **Wire host name.** Note the placeholder; decide separately. The Display
   PostScript analogy suggests a name evoking a display or a mirror rather than
   a wire, and it should not suggest kinship with `alfe`.

## 10. Acceptance criteria

- [ ] D1 exists, is backend-agnostic, and contains the fidelity matrix.
- [ ] D2 exists, with Part I semantics and Part II design, and reaches a stated
      decision on every item in §5 and §9.
- [ ] Every claim about AutoCAD or BricsCAD behaviour carries a provenance
      grade; every assumption is in the assumptions subsection.
- [ ] Every constraint C1–C8 is traceable to a section of D2 that satisfies it.
- [ ] The entity code lives in `cador`; the null host is `nihil` and contains
      only stubs.
- [ ] E1–E10 exist as runnable probes; those not yet run against a real product
      are marked pending, not passing.
- [ ] `alfe-test` runs green in GitLab CI against `cador` with no interactive
      input.

## 11. Out of scope

- Implementing the wire host, or naming it.
- Implementing the database backend chosen in §5.12 — this issue selects and
  specifies it.
- The lexical binding work, and the alpha-renaming and boxing strategies, except
  where R2 forces the symbol model to be per-document.
- GUI work of any kind.
